          text: 'Before leaving Cape Maclear, hike to the viewpoint above the village for panoramic views across the lake and islands. The trail starts behind the market area and climbs through dry miombo woodland for about 45 minutes. From the top, the view is breathtaking — the entire Cape Maclear peninsula stretches below with Thumbi Island, Domwe Island, and Mumbo Island dotting the turquoise water. You can see fishing boats, kayakers, and the sandy crescents of beaches you have explored. The early morning light is best for photographs and the climb is coolest before 9am.',
          tip: 'Ask a local guide to join you (2,000–3,000 MWK tip) — the trail is not well-marked and guides know the best viewpoints and can identify birds and plants.',
        },
        {
          time: 'Afternoon',
          icon: '☀️',
          title: 'Senga Bay or Nkhotakota Stopover',
          text: 'If heading north, break the journey at Senga Bay or the Nkhotakota Wildlife Reserve. Senga Bay is a lakeshore town with a more developed feel — there are proper restaurants, a market, and the beautiful Livingstonia Beach Hotel sits on the sand. Nkhotakota is Africa\'s oldest wildlife reserve and sees almost no tourists — walking safaris through riverine forest offer chances to spot elephants, sable antelope, and bushbuck. Both make excellent stopovers that show a different character of the lake. Local minibuses connect Cape Maclear to Monkey Bay, from where buses head north along the lakeshore.',
          tip: 'Minibuses from Cape Maclear to Monkey Bay cost around 1,500 MWK. From Monkey Bay, buses run north to Salima and Nkhotakota throughout the morning.',
        },
        {
          time: 'Evening',
          icon: '🌙',
          title: 'Final Lakeshore Sunset',
          text: 'Whether you are continuing north along the lake or heading to Lilongwe for onward travel, make time for one final swim and sunset. The warmth of the lake water, the vivid colours of the sky, and the friendly calls of fishermen heading out for the night create a farewell that stays with you long after you leave. Pick up some dried chambo and local honey from roadside vendors as gifts. Lake Malawi has a way of getting under your skin — most travellers who visit find themselves planning a return trip before they have even left the shore.',
          tip: 'Lilongwe is 3–4 hours from Monkey Bay by bus (3,000–5,000 MWK). AXA buses are the most reliable. Book a seat on the morning departure for the smoothest journey.',
